Ruijie Zhu · 103rd in this Competition · Posted 5 days ago
Stronger models barely beat Gemma 4 31B in this harness — teacher comparison on 124 public tasks
Hi all — sharing an experiment in case it saves someone time/money.

Question: is our score limited by the model, or by the harness setup? If a much stronger coding model (one that gets ~80% on SWE-bench Verified) runs through exactly the same agent config, how much better does it do?

Setup (identical for all models)

Same submission YAML (sample prompt, get_code_subgraph removed), same harness (swegemma 0.2.7, 2026-09-30 build), events compaction enabled as in the official notebook, 32k context.
Budget: 25 min / 50 tool calls per task, one task per process.
124 of the 129 public tasks. We fixed local test environments so that, for every one of these 124, the gold patch passes and an empty patch fails (5 tasks could not be made scorable locally).
Gemma: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct on vLLM 0.19.1, 2 independent passes.
Teachers via OpenRouter, routed through the same harness: DeepSeek V4 Pro, Qwen3.6-27B, 1 pass each.
Results

model	resolved	median minutes/task
Gemma 4 31B, pass 1	54/124	5.2
Gemma 4 31B, pass 2	53/123	5.2
DeepSeek V4 Pro	58/124	3.5
Qwen3.6-27B	59/123	3.9
The teachers are only +4–5 tasks ahead. For scale: between Gemma's two identical passes, 19 of 123 tasks (15%) flipped between solved and failed.
Of the 60 tasks Gemma failed in both passes, the two teachers combined solve only 10; 48 are solved by nobody. The teachers also fail 11–12 tasks that Gemma solved at least once.
Union of all 4 runs: 73/123.
Takeaways (our interpretation)

In this harness, the ceiling seems to come from the setup (32k context + compaction, call budget, sparse task descriptions — many are just "Fixes #NNNN"), not from raw model strength. Distilling a stronger teacher can't add much "new capability" here; the extra solves are few.
The teachers are noticeably faster (3.5–3.9 vs 5.2 min median). With ~5.5 min/task available in the 12 h official run, speed may matter more than extra capability.
Single runs are very noisy — be careful comparing prompt variants on small task batches.
Two harness gotchas we hit along the way

run_command is a synchronous tool, so it blocks the event loop. If you run several tasks concurrently in one eval process, one slow command (e.g. an agent's script hitting the 300 s timeout) freezes every task in that process, while their time budgets keep ticking. Run one task per process if you care about timing.
Gemma 4 edit_file calls whose old_string contains a """ docstring sometimes get mangled by vLLM's gemma4 tool-call parser (argument keys like <|"|>,filepath), so ADK rejects them with "mandatory input parameters are not present". The model then tends to retry the identical call many times (27× in one trace). Rejected calls don't count toward the tool-call cap, but they do cost time.
Happy to answer questions. Numbers are from a single pass per teacher, so treat ±5 tasks as noise.


